1
00:00:00,208 --> 00:00:02,522
So here for this course, just as in the

2
00:00:02,522 --> 00:00:04,595
previous section for LM Studio,

3
00:00:05,158 --> 00:00:07,311
I will use the Gemma 3 model, but you can of

4
00:00:07,343 --> 00:00:09,544
course play around with different models and

5
00:00:09,576 --> 00:00:11,167
use all kinds of models.

6
00:00:12,083 --> 00:00:15,233
Now, once you go to the model detail page of

7
00:00:15,233 --> 00:00:15,956
a given model,

8
00:00:16,566 --> 00:00:19,555
you have a dropdown here which allows you to

9
00:00:19,555 --> 00:00:22,994
choose from different flavors and

10
00:00:23,074 --> 00:00:25,468
quantizations of that model.

11
00:00:26,448 --> 00:00:30,207
All these identifiers here are called tags.

12
00:00:30,625 --> 00:00:33,902
So a given model, Gemma 3, has different

13
00:00:34,015 --> 00:00:37,757
tags that then point at different versions

14
00:00:37,934 --> 00:00:38,641
of that model.

15
00:00:39,573 --> 00:00:41,484
Now, the obvious tags are, of course, the

16
00:00:41,484 --> 00:00:44,022
tags that simply describe the different

17
00:00:44,135 --> 00:00:46,721
parameter sizes, so the different amounts of

18
00:00:46,785 --> 00:00:49,324
parameters, because the Gemma 3 model was

19
00:00:49,516 --> 00:00:52,488
published by Google with different amounts

20
00:00:52,488 --> 00:00:53,147
of parameters.

21
00:00:53,933 --> 00:00:55,985
But then we also got all these tags that

22
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:59,207
refer to different quantized versions of

23
00:00:59,207 --> 00:00:59,833
these models.

24
00:01:00,907 --> 00:01:03,857
The confusing part, however, is that also

25
00:01:03,953 --> 00:01:06,951
these versions here are quantized. It's just

26
00:01:06,999 --> 00:01:08,811
not obvious from the tag names.

27
00:01:09,805 --> 00:01:13,636
But by default, Ollama only has quantized

28
00:01:13,636 --> 00:01:16,779
versions in their model catalog because you

29
00:01:16,779 --> 00:01:19,232
typically want to run quantized versions on

30
00:01:19,264 --> 00:01:21,588
your system for all the reasons you learned

31
00:01:21,588 --> 00:01:23,480
about earlier in this course. You

32
00:01:23,528 --> 00:01:25,290
essentially have pretty much no

33
00:01:25,434 --> 00:01:28,014
disadvantages from using quantized versions,

34
00:01:28,238 --> 00:01:30,097
but you get better performance and lower

35
00:01:30,161 --> 00:01:31,330
hardware requirements.

36
00:01:32,179 --> 00:01:35,143
So therefore, these versions of the model

37
00:01:35,143 --> 00:01:37,739
that you find at the top here, these tags

38
00:01:38,075 --> 00:01:40,751
also point at quantized versions. You can

39
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:43,106
just think of them as the officially

40
00:01:43,362 --> 00:01:45,028
recommended versions.

41
00:01:45,685 --> 00:01:47,351
Because if you select one of these models,

42
00:01:47,464 --> 00:01:49,626
let's say the 12 billion parameter model,

43
00:01:50,251 --> 00:01:52,398
you find more information about it here. And

44
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:55,236
guess what? You also find quantization

45
00:01:55,236 --> 00:01:55,989
information here.

46
00:01:56,775 --> 00:01:58,491
So that proves that this

47
00:01:59,069 --> 00:02:00,865
version of the model with this tag

48
00:02:01,394 --> 00:02:04,425
also is quantized, which is a good thing, of

49
00:02:04,458 --> 00:02:04,746
course.

50
00:02:05,885 --> 00:02:07,777
You also find the size of the model here,

51
00:02:08,066 --> 00:02:10,440
which is the amount of disk space this model

52
00:02:10,456 --> 00:02:12,364
